    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Here Are Some of the Serious and Obscure Brands That EICMA 2026 Had To Offer – Worth Knowing About\nZFFCN's New UK Plant To Supply Gasoline, Hybrid, and Electric Vehicle Axles to Bentley\n13 photos\nPhoto: Bentley\nBentley is bringing a very important part of its vehicle assembly process closer to the British automaker's Crewe facility. ZF Foxconn Chassis Modules has started constructing a dedicated axle plant in Stoke-on-Trent, a facility that has been confirmed to supply a great deal of axles to the ultra-luxury automaker.\nProduction is scheduled to begin in 2027, and the ZFFCN axles in question are designed for a wide variety of vehicles. According to Bentley , the front and rear modules will support internal combustion, hybridized, and full-electric models. The Volkswagen Group-controlled manufacturer, however, did not mention any model whatsoever in the attached media release.\nIn any case, a joint venture that combines German chassis expertise with Taiwanese industrial capabilities is a truly interesting combo. The factory will occupy a site at Chatterley Park, which is approximately 17 miles (27 kilometers) from the Crewe headquarters. ZF Foxconn will assemble complete front and rear axle and suspension modules there.\nComplete modules will be shipped to Crewe's production lines through a just-in-sequence manufacturing arrangement. In other words, a specific axle should arrive at Bentley's factory right before the corresponding vehicle reaches the relevant assembly station. The alternative would be axles that sit idly in inventory until somebody needs them, and this alternative isn't particularly efficient.\nAs implied, ZF Foxconn Chassis Modules brings Germany's ZF Friedrichshafen AG and Taiwan's Hon Hai Technology Group together. The partners completed the transaction that birthed the 50:50 joint venture in April 2024, with Foxconn acquiring half of ZF Chassis Modules GmbH.\nPhoto: Bentley In ZF Foxconn's own words, the company should be viewed as a full-service automotive manufacturing and logistics provider. It specializes in chassis systems, modules, and assemblies for both volume and premium automakers. As for its work, ZFFCN covers axle assembly, corner modules, supplier development, industrialization, quality management, and supply chain operations.\nZFFCN brings more than 30 years of experience in assembling axle systems and operating JIT and JIS production facilities. The biggest difference between just-in-time production and the JIS system is how the desired parts are presented to the contracting manufacturer. JIT means the parts are delivered to the production line when they are needed, while JIS delivers them when needed and in the exact assembly order required by the assembly line.\nAs of this writing, ZFFCN employs approximately 4,000 souls across 27 locations worldwide. The joint venture's experience should prove immensely useful at Crewe, where Bentley has to accommodate a growing number of powertrain technologies without creating one too many distinct supply arrangements for every model family.\nBentley's current lineup comprises four core models. The Continental GT, which can be had with a canvas top, is joined by the mechanically similar Flying Spur ultra-luxury sedan. The Bentayga is more than a Volkswagen Touareg with a nice suit, and the same can be said about the Porsche Cayenne Electric-derived Torcal electric sport utility vehicle.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBentley Foxconn ZF production industry\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT You’ll Never Believe How Much The F15 BMW X5 Now Costs. It’s Pennies on the Dollar!",
